   "l": "Heaven-and-earth: not humane —\ntaking the ten-thousand things as straw-dogs;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Heaven and earth are not humane: they treat all things as straw dogs.",
   "n": "以 A 為 B — 'take A and make it B', so 'treat A as B'. You will meet this frame constantly; 為 here is wéi, 'to make, to act as' (read wèi it would mean 'for the sake of'). 仁 is the cardinal Confucian virtue, kindness towards others, and the line is aimed straight at it: heaven and earth play no favourites. 芻狗: the Zhuangzi describes straw dogs plaited for a sacrifice, decked out and honoured while the rite lasts, then thrown out and trodden on — that is the usual reading here. Wang Bi, whose text this is, takes the two characters apart instead: heaven does not grow fodder (芻) for the beasts, yet the beasts eat it; it does not make dogs (狗) for men, yet men eat them. Either way the charge is indifference, not cruelty.",
   "p": true
  },
