   "l": "The sage: not humane —\ntaking the hundred-surnames as straw-dogs.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The sage is not humane: he treats the people as straw dogs.",
   "n": "An exact parallel: as heaven and earth stand to the ten thousand things, so the sage stands to the people. 百姓 is 'the hundred surnames' — the clans, and so everybody. 聖人 here is not a moral saint but the ruler who holds the Way; his want of 仁 is a refusal to prefer one person to another, and 'the sage is not humane' was written to sting a Confucian ear."
