   "l": "The space between heaven-and-earth —\nsurely it is like a bellows?\nEmpty, and yet not used up;\nset moving, and all the more comes forth.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The space between heaven and earth — is it not like a bellows? Hollow, yet it never gives out; set it working, and more and more comes out of it.",
   "n": "其…乎 frames a guess rather than a statement: 其 here is not the possessive 'its' of chapter 1 but a particle of supposition, and 乎 at the end turns the line into a question — 'surely it is…?' A 橐籥 is a forge bellows, a leather bag (橐) driving air down a tube (籥); it works precisely because it is hollow, and the harder it is worked the more it gives. 屈 and 出 rhymed in Old Chinese; in Mandarin (qū / chū) they come close and just miss."
  },
