   "l": "Much speech, again and again at a dead end —\nnot equal to keeping to the centre.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Much talking runs dry again and again; better to keep to the centre.",
   "n": "數 is read shuò here, 'repeatedly' — the same word as in the Analects' 事君數, to importune a lord — not shù 'number'. 不如 X is the ordinary comparison, 'does not come up to X', hence 'better to X'. The Mawangdui manuscripts read 多聞 'much hearing, much learning' where the received text has 多言 'much speaking', which moves the target from talk to erudition. 窮 qióng and 中 zhōng rhyme — and this is one of the rhymes you can still hear in Mandarin."
